<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asano Nagaakira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1586–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asano Nagaakira was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, son of Asano Nagamasa. In 1619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> transferred him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, with about 426,000 koku, after the dismissal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a>. His descendants ruled the Hiroshima Domain, one of the largest in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, until the abolition of the domains in 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asano-nagaakira.jpg" alt="Asano Nagaakira" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagaakira_Asano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
